    {
      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "To find the midpoint of segment \\( s_2 \\), we first need to determine the coordinates of the endpoints of segment \\( s_2 \\). Segment \\( s_2 \\) is obtained by translating segment \\( s_1 \\) by 2 units to the right and 3 units up. \n\nFirst, let's identify the endpoints of segment \\( s_1 \\). The endpoints of \\( s_1 \\) are \\((4,1)\\) and \\((-8,5)\\).\n\nThe translation involves adding 2 units to the x-coordinate and 3 units to the y-coordinate of each endpoint. So, we will calculate the new coordinates for both endpoints after the transformation.\n\nNew x-coordinate:\n\\[ 4 + 2 = 6 \\]\n\\[ -8 + 2 = -6 \\]\n\nNew y-coordinate:\n\\[ 1 + 3 = 4 \\]\n\\[ 5 + 3 = 8 \\]\n\nSo, the endpoints of segment \\( s_2 \\) are \\((6,4)\\) and \\((-6,8)\\).\n\nNext, we will find the midpoint of segment \\( s_2 \\). The formula for the midpoint \\( M \\) of a segment with endpoints \\( (x_1, y_1) \\) and \\( (x_2, y_2) \\) is:\n\n\\[\nM = \\left( \\frac{x_1 + x_2}{2}, \\frac{y_1 + y_2}{2} \\right)\n\\]\n\nSubstituting the coordinates of \\( s_2 \\) into this formula:\n\n\\[\nM = \\left( \\frac{6 + (-6)}{2}, \\frac{4 + 8}{2} \\right)\n\\]\n\nSimplify the calculations:\n\n\\[\nM = \\left( \\frac{0}{2}, \\frac{12}{2} \\right)\n\\]\n\n\\[\nM = (0, 6)\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the midpoint of segment \\( s_2 \\) is \\(\\boxed{(0,6)}\\).",
